// This file is DELIBERATELY BROKEN -- it will not verify, on purpose.
//
// `x + y == 10 && x >= 0 && y >= 0` is satisfiable -- x = 10, y = 0 is one
// witness among infinitely many -- so this looks like exactly the kind of
// fact `:|` exists for. It still fails:
//
//   [Verification Error] The right-hand side of this bind statement may not
//   hold
//
// `:|`'s existence check is an ordinary SMT proof obligation, not an oracle:
// the solver has to actually find (or be pointed at) a witness, and a
// compound proposition over several inequalities isn't something it
// searches for on spec alone, the same way it won't invent an induction
// hypothesis for `assert ... with` unhelped. Compare Part 4's own use of
// `:|` (`hit_counter_ghost.rav`'s `v2 :| own(c.count, v2, 1.0)`), which
// reads its witness straight off an `own` fact already in scope -- see the
// tutorial text for why that's the reliable shape, not the shape above.
proc demo()
{
  ghost var x: Int
  ghost var y: Int
  x, y :| x + y == 10 && x >= 0 && y >= 0
}
